.Dd June 8, 2026
.Dt CAP_XFER_LIMIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_xfer_limit
.Nd limit descriptor transfer state
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/capsicum.h
.Ft int
.Fn cap_xfer_limit "int fd" "int state"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn cap_xfer_limit
system call restricts whether a file descriptor can be transferred to
another process via
.Xr sendmsg 2
with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
control messages or via mac_capability messaging.
.Pp
The transfer state is orthogonal to capability rights set by
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 .
Calls to
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_rights_clear 3 ,
and
.Dv CAP_ALL
do not affect the transfer state.
.Pp
The authority conveyed by a permitted transfer can be independently
attenuated with
.Xr cap_xfer_rights_limit 2 .
.Pp
The
.Fa state
argument must be one of the following values:
.Bl -tag -width CAP_XFER_UNLIMITED
.It Dv CAP_XFER_UNLIMITED
No transfer restriction.
This is the default state for newly created descriptors.
A multi-hop delegation is expressed by attenuating the descriptor to
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
before each forwarding transfer, rather than a kernel-tracked hop budget.
.It Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
The descriptor may be transferred exactly once.
After a successful transfer, the state automatically transitions to
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE .
.It Dv CAP_XFER_NONE
The descriptor cannot be transferred.
.El
.Pp
Transfer states are monotonically decreasing: a descriptor's state can
only be tightened, never relaxed.
The ordering is
.Dv CAP_XFER_UNLIMITED
\*(Gt
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
\*(Gt
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE .
Setting the same state again is permitted and has no effect.
.Pp
The transfer state is inherited across
.Xr dup 2
and
.Xr fork 2 .
Closing and reopening the underlying file resets the state to
.Dv CAP_XFER_UNLIMITED .
.Pp
This system call is allowed in capability mode
.Pq see Xr cap_enter 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Prevent a file descriptor from being passed to another process:
.Bd -literal
#include <sys/capsicum.h>

int fd = open("/tmp/secret", O_RDONLY);
if (cap_xfer_limit(fd, CAP_XFER_NONE) < 0)
	err(1, "cap_xfer_limit");
/* fd can still be used locally but cannot be sent via SCM_RIGHTS */
.Ed
.Pp
Allow exactly one transfer:
.Bd -literal
cap_xfer_limit(fd, CAP_XFER_ONCE);
/* First sendmsg with SCM_RIGHTS succeeds and exhausts the state */
/* Second sendmsg with the same fd fails with ENOTCAPABLE */
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn cap_xfer_limit
succeeds unless:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBADF
The
.Fa fd
argument is not a valid active descriptor.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa state
argument is not a valid transfer state.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The requested
.Fa state
is less restrictive than the current transfer state.
Transfer states can only be tightened, never relaxed.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
The running kernel was compiled without
.Cd "options CAPABILITY_MODE" .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_xfer_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr sendmsg 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr rights 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn cap_xfer_limit
system call first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
